{"name":"Rolling Stone","hex":"#6d7876","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Rolling Stone","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of the members of the Rolling Stones."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Rolling_Stone"}],"entries":[{"title":"Rolling Stone","blocks":[{"text":"Rolling Stone is an American monthly magazine that focuses on music, politics, and popular culture. It was founded in San Francisco, California, in 1967 by Jann Wenner and the music critic Ralph J. Gleason. It is described as left-leaning and liberal."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/d/df/Rolling_Stone_1000th_Issue_May_June_2006.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rolling_Stone"},{"title":"Rolling","blocks":[{"text":"Rolling is a type of motion that combines rotation and translation of that object with respect to a surface, such that, if ideal conditions exist, the two are in contact with each other without sliding."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Rolling_animation.gif/330px-Rolling_animation.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rolling"},{"title":"Rock (geology)","blocks":[{"text":"In geology, a rock is any naturally occurring solid mass or aggregate of minerals or mineraloid matter. It is categorized by the minerals included, its chemical composition, and the way in which it is formed. Rocks form the Earth's outer solid layer, the crust, and most of its interior, except for the liquid outer core and pockets of magma in the asthenosphere. The study of rocks involves multiple subdisciplines of geology, including petrology and mineralogy. It may be limited to rocks found on Earth, or it may include planetary geology that studies the rocks of other celestial objects."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Marsh_Butte_and_Geikie_Peak%2C_Grand_Canyon.jpg/330px-Marsh_Butte_and_Geikie_Peak%2C_Grand_Canyon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rock_(geology)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/RS_500_Front_Cover.jpg/330px-RS_500_Front_Cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/RS_500_Front_Cover.jpg/330px-RS_500_Front_Cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Rolling Stone cover from 2004","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARS_500_Front_Cover.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a1/Moglfm2207_rodadura.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a1/Moglfm2207_rodadura.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMoglfm2207_rodadura.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Kummakivi_balancing_rock_in_Ruokolahti%2C_Finland.jpg/500px-Kummakivi_balancing_rock_in_Ruokolahti%2C_Finland.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Kummakivi_balancing_rock_in_Ruokolahti%2C_Finland.jpg/1280px-Kummakivi_balancing_rock_in_Ruokolahti%2C_Finland.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Kummakivi_balancing_rock_in_Ruokolahti%2C_Finland.jpg/500px-Kummakivi_balancing_rock_in_Ruokolahti%2C_Finland.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A balancing rock called Kummakivi (literally \"strange stone\")[3]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%